---
/**
 * /analytical-governance — THE CURRENT v2.0 DOORWAY (authored 2026-08-26 under the CG2 final E rulings of 15:59).
 *
 * AUTHORED FROM THE RECORD, NOT EDITED FROM THE COMPRESSION (item 5: author the current route fresh from v2.0). The page that stood here
 * from 21 August compressed Analytical Governance v1.1. v2.0 did not reword that argument, it
 * reorganised the category: the subject is now the LEGITIMACY OF THE ANALYTICAL SERVICE, servability
 * is a gate inside it rather than the thesis, and the worked example's own numbers changed. So this
 * file is new, and the v1.1 body is preserved unedited at /history/analytical-governance-v1-1 —
 * created FIRST, deliberately, so no moment existed in which it was unreachable (item 10: historical route first).
 *
 * SOURCE OF RECORD. The deposited paper, Analytical Governance v2.0, is canonical. This page may
 * compress, sequence and connect it; it does not extend it. Every section names the section of the
 * record it comes from in the comment above it, and the three places the page speaks in its own voice
 * are marked *page voice*.
 *
 * ORDER IS RULED (item 7): service relationship → legitimacy → intent gap → servability gap →
 * responses → STANDING → serving ↔ authoring → governed crossings → conformance. Standing sits after
 * the responses because it is downstream of the serving verdict: it governs what a SERVED result may
 * carry across the next boundary.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT HERE, BY RULING (E.9). The capability/legitimacy matrix, the three powers, and "If it
 * cannot refuse, it is not governed" are v1.1-only. v2.0 supplies no grounding for any of them, none
 * was invented, and all three are preserved on the historical route. Two v1.1 conformance
 * blockquotes went with them.
 *
 * THE WORD "CORRECT" IS AUDITED (item 3, as refined at 16:04). The governing principle:
 *
 *     Correctness is always correctness with respect to something.
 *
 * A result may be completely correct under a NARROWLY DEFINED operation, query, derivation or
 * analytical object without thereby being established as the legitimate answer to the user's
 * analytical purpose. So the page never says "correct number" or "correct result" where it means
 * "the answer the user should receive"; it names the predicate actually being asserted —
 * arithmetically correct, correctly produced by this query, lawfully derived, support-sufficient,
 * reproducible, servable, authorized, legitimate as the answer.
 *
 * "Narrowly defined" is DESCRIPTIVE. Neither "narrow correctness" nor "local correctness" appears
 * anywhere on this page or in these files: coining one would create the formal concept the ruling
 * declines, and would hand a reader a term to use instead of the predicate. A precision rule, not a
 * taxonomy — and the definition strip is what makes it hold, because `result` and `answer` are
 * defined where a reader meets them.
 *
 * THE THEORY OF CERTAINTY IS A POINTER, TWICE (item 8): the upstream discipline named in
 * §Legitimacy, and a relation in §Where this sits. None of its content — no grounds, no substitution
 * error, no reach, no composition, no exposure — and no domain triad, named or implied.
 *
 * PUBLICATION FACTS. Registry-derived, everywhere, for both AG and ToC. No version string and no DOI
 * is typed into this file — check_publications.py classifies it `derived` and fails the build if one
 * appears. No count of works, papers or domains is rendered (G10).
 *
 * ANCHORS. #two-gaps, #can-may and #powers have no successor here and are NOT faked into existence to
 * preserve a name (item 6); the shim at the bottom forwards exactly those three fragments to the
 * same anchor on the historical route. #serving → #responses, #boundaries → #crossings and
 * #authorize → #path are in-page renames and resolve locally.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ServiceHero from '../components/ag/ServiceHero.astro';
import ServiceWound from '../components/ag/ServiceWound.astro';
import TermStrip from '../components/ag/TermStrip.astro';
import ServingBoundary from '../components/ag/ServingBoundary.astro';
import WireQuotation from '../components/ag/WireQuotation.astro';
import { citation } from '../data/publications';

const paper = citation('w-analytical-governance');
const certainty = citation('w-theory-of-certainty');
// The applied paper. Label derived; the route is the deposited work's reading surface.
const agent = citation('w-do-not-let-your-ai-agent-govern-itself');

// §2 · the three obligations by which legitimacy is maintained through the passage (v2.0 §2.1–2.3).
const OBLIGATIONS = [
  {
    id: 'faithfulness',
    name: 'Faithfulness',
    formal: 'purpose → request → answer',
    text:
      'Does the service preserve the user’s purpose as it becomes an analytical request and an answer? Two duties, and only one of them is translation. Fidelity prevents the system from saying something different from what the user means. Enablement helps the user say more precisely what they are trying to know.',
    guard: 'Neither authorizes the system to choose silently on the user’s behalf.',
  },
  {
    id: 'reliability',
    name: 'Reliability',
    formal: 'established object → produced result',
    text:
      'Can the governed analytical process produce the established result consistently under the relevant conditions? Data availability, state, execution, materialization, reproducibility. Reliability does not create analytical meaning; it preserves and produces what was already formulated and established.',
    guard: 'A reliable production path must reproduce and carry forward the object that was actually established.',
  },
  {
    id: 'certainty',
    name: 'Certainty sufficient for reliance',
    formal: 'grounds → warranted reliance',
    text:
      'Are the grounds relevant to this result established, preserved and respected as the service crosses from request to answer, and from answer to use? The sufficiency of a ground depends on the exposure the reliance creates.',
    guard: 'This is where Analytical Governance defers: the discipline of grounds is developed elsewhere.',
  },
];

// §8 · where this sits. Deliberately a relation list, not a symmetric four-box diagram: these bodies
// of law have different shapes, and a diagram that made them look parallel would claim a symmetry
// the corpus does not have. (v2.0 §11.)
const NEIGHBOURS = [
  {
    id: 'data-governance',
    name: 'Data governance',
    relation: 'composes with',
    text:
      'Data governance governs the stewardship, access, quality and control of data assets. Analytical Governance governs whether the analytical service is faithfully formulated, sufficiently established, honestly served and appropriately used. The two compose; neither substitutes for the other.',
    canonical: true,
  },
  {
    id: 'tod',
    name: 'The Theory of Data',
    href: '/learn/what-is-the-theory-of-data',
    relation: 'supplies interior law to',
    text:
      'Laws of governed analytical identity, derivability and consistency. It is narrowly that: not a general ontology of analytical objects, and not the law of every world the service crosses.',
  },
  {
    id: 'bridge',
    name: 'The Statistical Bridge',
    href: '/evidence',
    relation: 'governs a crossing inside',
    text:
      'Passages from governed evidence through formal inference to licensed claims. Analytical Governance may compose that crossing into a larger analytical path. It does not replace the Bridge’s law, and it does not adjudicate inference itself.',
  },
  {
    id: 'certainty',
    // NOT DERIVED, AND DELIBERATELY SO (Huayin, 2026-08-26). This literal was briefly replaced
    // with `certainty.label` when the paper was retitled to The Ground for Certainty, on the
    // reasoning that a title is as much a publication fact as a version or a DOI and this file
    // types none of those. That reasoning was right about publication facts and wrong about THIS
    // STRING, which is not one.
    //
    //     "Theory of Certainty is still valid, except when citing the article, the title needs to
    //      match the new paper. Not all Theory of Relativity are in books titled Theory of
    //      Relativity."
    //
    // The relation this row carries is `supplies the upstream discipline to`. It names the
    // DISCIPLINE, not the deposit — the same way a sentence about relativity names relativity and
    // not whichever paper currently states it best. A theory's name is editorial and stable; a
    // publication's title moves when the author retitles it, and those are different facts that
    // happen to have been the same string until today.
    //
    // The href stays registry-derived, so the link still lands on the current record. Anchor text
    // that names a theory and a target that resolves to its current foundational publication is
    // what a citation to a theory has always looked like, and it is not a mismatch.
    //
    // The PUBLICATION is named once, one screen up, in §Legitimacy — "developed separately in
    // {certainty.label}" — which IS a citation of the article and therefore does re-resolve. Two
    // appearances, two different jobs, and item 8 of the page rulings caps it there.
    name: 'The Theory of Certainty',
    href: certainty.href,
    external: true,
    relation: 'supplies the upstream discipline to',
    text:
      'What grounds carry a conclusion, what they establish, where their warrant stops, and whether they can bear the reliance being placed on them. Analytical Governance turns those questions into a governed analytical service; it does not restate them.',
  },
  {
    id: 'columna',
    name: 'Columna',
    href: '/columna',
    relation: 'is an executable consequence of',
    text:
      'Evidence that these distinctions can be embodied in a working system. Not the definition of the category, and the category does not depend on it.',
  },
  {
    id: 'frameql',
    name: 'Frame-QL',
    href: '/what-is-frameql',
    relation: 'is one request language for',
    text:
      'Convenient as a request boundary because it speaks natively in governed analytical objects. No language or protocol is required by the category — only that every identity-bearing distinction be explicit in the request or uniquely derivable from the governed model.',
  },
];

// §9 · the minimum a conforming practice or architecture needs (v2.0 §12), verbatim in substance.
const REQUIREMENTS = [
  'a way to preserve user purpose while translating human or machine intent into an explicit analytical request;',
  'a governed analytical representation capable of independently establishing identity, derivation, support requirements and relevant state;',
  'a reliable production path that preserves what was analytically established;',
  'a servability gate capable of withholding or qualifying analytical permission;',
  'a way to preserve standing, conditions, reasons and alternatives as results cross boundaries;',
  'separation between analytical establishment and later cost, security, application and authorization decisions;',
  'execution faithful to what governance actually authorized.',
];

const CLOSING = [
  'Preserve purpose.',
  'Establish the analytical object and its grounds.',
  'Produce it reliably.',
  'Serve only what is servable.',
  'Preserve standing across crossings.',
  'Govern consequential use.',
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Analytical Governance — datumwise"
  description="Producing a result is not the same as serving an answer. Analytical Governance governs the legitimacy of the analytical service — the intent gap, the servability gap, standing, and the boundary at which a governed system may refuse."
  wide={true}
  chip={false}
>
  <ServiceHero />
  <ServiceWound />
  <TermStrip />

  {/* §2 · LEGITIMACY (v2.0 §2, §2.1–2.3). The spine is the paper's operational cut, set as one line
      of type because it is the only thing on the page that is a sequence. */}
  <section id="must-remain-true" class="legit ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="legit-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="legit-h" class="ds-label">Legitimacy &mdash; what must remain true</h2>

      <p class="passage">
        User purpose <span class="arw">&rarr;</span> analytical intent <span class="arw">&rarr;</span>
        governed analytical construction <span class="arw">&rarr;</span> reliable production
        <span class="arw">&rarr;</span> servability <span class="arw">&rarr;</span> warranted reliance
        <span class="arw">&rarr;</span> consequential use
      </p>

      <p class="legit-lede ds-body">
        This is an operational cut through the analytical service, not a replacement taxonomy for the
        foundational domains whose laws the service uses. Different systems may combine functions.
        Governance requires that the distinctions remain visible enough to adjudicate.
      </p>

      <ol class="obligations">
        {OBLIGATIONS.map((o) => (
          <li class="obligation" id={`obligation-${o.id}`}>
            <h3 class="ob-name">{o.name}</h3>
            <p class="ob-formal">{o.formal}</p>
            <p class="ob-text">{o.text}</p>
            <p class="ob-guard">{o.guard}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      {/* THE THEORY OF CERTAINTY, appearance 1 of 2. A pointer, not a summary: none of its content
          appears on this page, and no triad of domains is named or implied (item 8). */}
      <p class="defer">
        Analytical Governance does not develop its own account of what makes grounds sufficient. That
        upstream discipline &mdash; what grounds carry a conclusion, what they establish, where their
        warrant stops, and whether they can bear the reliance being placed on them &mdash; is
        developed separately in
        <a href={certainty.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{certainty.label}</a>, and Analytical
        Governance turns those questions into a governed analytical service.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* §3 · THE INTENT GAP (v2.0 §3). The worked case carries the section: it is the shortest proof
      that enablement is not translation. */}
  <section id="intent-gap" class="gap ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="intent-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="intent-h" class="ds-label">The intent gap</h2>
      <p class="ds-proposition">From purpose to governed request.</p>

      <div class="gap-body">
        <p class="ds-body">
          The first gap is the distance between what a user can initially express and the analytical
          request that faithfully captures their purpose with the distinctions governance needs.
          Closing it has two obligations: preserve the purpose, and enable better articulation.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          AI and semantic systems can search vocabulary, retrieve definitions, expose governed
          distinctions, propose interpretations, explain alternatives, and formulate a candidate
          governed request. <strong>This is not merely translation.</strong> A good analytical service
          can expand what a user is able to say, by making the relevant distinctions available to be
          said.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="worked">
          <p>
            A user asks for &ldquo;average revenue.&rdquo; The service can surface the governed
            alternatives &mdash; among reporting stores, among existing stores, among stores that were
            open &mdash; explain the difference, and let the user choose.
          </p>
          <p class="worked-turn">
            That is enablement. Silently selecting one of them is unauthorized formulation by the
            serving system.
          </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p class="ds-body">
          Enablement must not conceal materially relevant governed alternatives. If the service
          surfaces a selective set rather than the relevant governed set, that selectivity must itself
          be governed or disclosed.
        </p>
        <p class="gap-close">
          The intent gap is closed when the user&rsquo;s purpose has been formulated &mdash; through
          preservation and, where useful, enablement &mdash; as an analytical request explicit enough
          for <strong>independent adjudication</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* §4 · THE SERVABILITY GAP (v2.0 §4). The sentence that used to be this page's thesis lands here,
      in its own edition's words. */}
  <section id="servability" class="gap servability ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="serv-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="serv-h" class="ds-label">The servability gap</h2>
      <p class="ds-proposition">Faithful request does not imply servable request.</p>

      <div class="gap-body">
        <p class="equation">Servable = Support Sufficient <span class="and">AND</span> Analytically Established</p>

        <p class="ds-body">
          <strong>Analytical establishment</strong> asks whether the requested analytical object exists
          under the governed analytical model and whether the requested derivation is lawful.
          <strong>Support sufficiency</strong> asks whether the evidence and sufficient state this
          particular request requires are presently available.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          A missing feed can leave a lawful request unsupported. An unlawful reduction can leave
          abundant data unable to establish the requested result. An unresolved population can make a
          computable denominator analytically unestablished.
        </p>

        <p class="pillar">Computability does not imply servability.</p>

        <p class="ds-body">
          Servability is not a generic property of a dataset, metric, model or platform. It is a
          determination about a particular analytical request under current grounds. And a result may
          be servable and still not be served: servability establishes the analytical permission for
          the requested answer, while authorization, risk and disclosure conditions determine the
          serving outcome.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          Time has to be typed carefully. A late-arriving or retroactively corrected record does not
          necessarily change the identity of the analytical object; it may instead change whether the
          required state was support-sufficient when the answer was served. If the governed definition
          itself is versioned over time, analytical identity may change too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ServingBoundary />

  {/* §6 · STANDING (v2.0 §7), placed after the responses by ruling: standing is downstream of the
      serving verdict. Risk and authority (§8) fold in as its second movement — the same boundary
      seen from the other side. */}
  <section id="standing" class="standing ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="standing-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="standing-h" class="ds-label">Standing</h2>
      <p class="ds-proposition">What may the served result now be treated as?</p>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <p class="ds-body">
            Servability asks whether a candidate result may be served as the answer. Consequential use
            creates a further question, and it is not the same one.
          </p>
          <p class="ds-body">
            Standing is claim- and boundary-specific. A result may stand for exploratory display and
            not for compensation. A statistical estimate may stand as an estimate and not as a causal
            claim. A result may require disclosure before publication.
          </p>
          <p class="pillar">
            Standing prevents a result from becoming more authoritative merely because it travelled.
            Conclusions are portable; their grounds are less so.
          </p>
          <p class="ds-body">
            This is the wound at the top of the page, one boundary later. There, a value correct under
            a narrower definition was not yet the answer to the question asked. Here, an answer that
            was entitled to be served is not thereby entitled to everything that might be done with
            it. In both cases what must not travel unexamined is the authority of the result, and what
            fixes its limit is the grounds and the definition that earned it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="col-label">Risk and authority come after analytical establishment</p>
          <p class="ds-body">
            Cost, security, application risk and authorization can constrain an otherwise established
            analytical service. They cannot supply a missing analytical ground.
          </p>
          <blockquote class="rule-quote">
            <p>Risk may constrain the right answer. It cannot turn an unestablished answer into the right one.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <p class="ds-body">
            An unresolved analytical identity, an unsupported population or an unlawful derivation is
            not a higher-risk version of the requested answer. Once the object and its required support
            are established, exposure matters: the same result may be acceptable for exploration and
            unacceptable for compensation, publication or automated action.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* §7 · SERVING ↔ AUTHORING (v2.0 §6). *Page voice* in the last line only: the page states the
      requirement that an authoring process exist and be separate. It does not imply datumwise ships
      one, and no forward reference or roadmap language appears. */}
  <section id="authoring" class="authoring ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="auth-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="auth-h" class="ds-label">The governed world is authored, not assumed</h2>
      <p class="ds-proposition">Serving applies the constitution. Authoring changes it.</p>

      <p class="auth-lede ds-body">
        Analytical service operates against a governed <strong>constitution</strong>: analytical
        meaning, law, support requirements, authority. That constitution must be declared, reviewed and
        ratified through a process separate from serving. Authoring changes or extends it through an
        authority event that can be reviewed and ratified.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="conf-sound">
        <p>A serving system must not silently create the missing meaning or authority that would make its own answer servable.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <ol class="loop">
        <li>unresolved need</li>
        <li>authoring or declaration</li>
        <li>review and ratification</li>
        <li>governed constitution</li>
        <li>serving</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="pillar">Clarify stays inside the constitution. Escalate reaches the constitution&rsquo;s edge.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* §8 · GOVERNED CROSSINGS (v2.0 §9) and where this sits (v2.0 §11). */}
  <section id="crossings" class="crossings ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="cross-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="cross-h" class="ds-label">Govern the crossings</h2>

      <ol class="crossing-list">
        <li>purpose becomes a request</li>
        <li>a request becomes a plan</li>
        <li>a plan produces a result</li>
        <li>a result becomes an answer</li>
        <li>an answer becomes action</li>
      </ol>

      <p class="cross-lede ds-body">
        Analytical service becomes consequential at crossings, and each crossing is an opportunity for
        authority to travel farther than its grounds.
      </p>

      <p class="pillar">
        The component that can produce a candidate result need not be the component entitled to
        authorize what that result becomes.
      </p>

      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <p class="ds-body">
            AI agents are <strong>one application</strong> of this crossing discipline. A model may
            interpret language, formulate a candidate governed request, generate candidate SQL,
            summarize evidence, or explain an adjudication result. None of those capabilities requires
            the model itself to carry analytical authority.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="ds-body">
            A structural execution boundary can close one consequential path independently of
            predictions about the agent. This is the role of a <strong>blast wall</strong>: a
            structural boundary that prevents reasoning output from becoming consequential execution
            directly. The same principle applies to serving &mdash; the agent may propose or explain;
            the governed serving boundary determines what the result is entitled to become.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 id="where-this-sits" class="sub-label">Where this sits</h3>
      <p class="bound-lede ds-body">
        Analytics is not governed by one body of law. Analytical Governance coordinates these kinds of
        interior law without replacing them: it governs the service passage &mdash; what must remain
        true as purpose becomes request, request becomes result, result becomes answer, and answer
        becomes consequential use. Its subject includes process and practice as well as architecture,
        because analysts, data systems, semantic systems, AI agents, reviewers and execution controls
        all have to preserve analytical meaning and authority across handoffs.
      </p>

      <dl class="neighbours">
        {NEIGHBOURS.map((n) => (
          <div class="neighbour" id={`boundary-${n.id}`}>
            <dt>
              {n.href
                ? <a class="n-name" href={n.href} rel={n.external ? 'noopener' : undefined}
                     target={n.external ? '_blank' : undefined}>{n.name}</a>
                : <span class="n-name">{n.name}</span>}
              <span class="n-rel">{n.relation} analytical governance</span>
            </dt>
            <dd class={n.canonical ? 'canonical' : ''}>{n.text}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <p class="read-on">
        <a href="/research">The research corpus</a>
        <a href="/positions/never-let-your-agent-touch-the-database">The authority boundary, argued</a>
        <a href="/the-argument#exhibit-b">Exhibit B &mdash; four requests, four earned verdicts</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* §9 · CONFORMANCE (v2.0 §12). The membership test, argued as requirements a reader can hold their
      own architecture against — followed by the non-claims, which are what keep this a category
      rather than a product pitch. */}
  <section id="conformance" class="conformance ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="conf-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="conf-h" class="ds-label">Conformance</h2>
      <p class="ds-proposition">What a conforming service needs, at minimum.</p>

      <ol class="reqs">
        {REQUIREMENTS.map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
      </ol>

      <div class="nonclaims">
        <p class="col-label">And what the framework does not claim</p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          It does not guarantee perfect intent, perfect data, perfect governed declarations or perfect
          decisions. It does not replace semantic knowledge, statistical inference, security
          engineering, physical optimization or decision governance. It requires no particular query
          language, semantic layer or implementation architecture.
        </p>
        <p class="ds-body">
          It requires that the analytical target, the grounds relevant to serving it, and the authority
          for consequential use be <strong>independently adjudicable somewhere</strong> before the
          service crosses the relevant boundary.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <WireQuotation />

  {/* CLOSING (v2.0 §13). Returns to the service, not to product marketing. */}
  <section id="path" class="closing ds ds-paper ds-section" aria-labelledby="close-h">
    <div class="ds-measure-wide">
      <h2 id="close-h" class="ds-label">The discipline</h2>
      <ol class="path">
        {CLOSING.map((step) => <li>{step}</li>)}
      </ol>
      <p class="closing-line">
        The goal is not to formalize every analytical act. It is to ensure that when analytics serves a
        user, the answer remains faithful to the purpose that called for it and carries no more
        authority than it has earned.
      </p>
      <div class="paths">
        <p class="path-out">
          <span class="p-label">Read the paper</span>
          <a href={paper.href} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{paper.label} &mdash; Version {paper.version}</a>
          <span class="p-meta">DOI {paper.doi}</span>
        </p>
        {/* APPLIED, NOT NEIGHBOURING (2026-08-28). Deliberately a `path-out` and NOT a row in
            NEIGHBOURS above: that section relates FRAMEWORKS to this one — data governance, ToD,
            the Bridge — and a paper that applies the framework is a different kind of object. It
            sits here, where the page is already telling a reader where to go next. */}
        <p class="path-out">
          <span class="p-label">Applied to AI agents</span>
          <a href="/read/do-not-let-your-ai-agent-govern-itself">{agent.label} &rarr;</a>
          <span class="p-meta">the intent and servability gaps, put to work on agent authority</span>
        </p>
        <p class="path-out">
          <span class="p-label">See the executable consequence</span>
          <a href="/columna">Columna &rarr;</a>
          <span class="p-meta">the same boundaries, in a system that ships</span>
        </p>
        <p class="path-out">
          <span class="p-label">The previous doorway</span>
          <a href="/history/analytical-governance-v1-1">Analytical Governance v1.1 &rarr;</a>
